body:
@usradam don't recycle one of the PRs: Just make a new one. If you recycle one, the prior comments on them will confuse me into thinking they are still about communications or one of the existing others. It is easier if I see there were 3 PRs that got combined into a new one, than 2 closed and 1 existing one modified. Does that make sense?

Please make sure the commits in these 3 PRs that were meant to be individual are left in a state you didn't carry functions from one to the other or that will confuse me. 

The engagement_sentiment() question item 2: Post another comment for me here clarifying what the PRs and functions are. I thought you were adding commits to Haotian's existing sentiment notebook Rmd PR on his fork. Are you not? (also please reference functions as blabla() rather than blabla,  and notebooks as .Rmd, I can't tell for certain otherwise)

@jseto808 

Are you able to sanity test this for me: You currently should have a transform that can take author-author communication. Is it doable for you to get any existing config, download some communication data from anywhere, and try to use your export to create an author-author (meaning you need to apply a bipartite or temporal projection) on it and load on DV8? You can then use the GUI to run the DR Space clustering. Or better yet, use the associated dv8_ notebook to do the clustering for you all within Kaiaulu. 

This is a good use case test for you to check on a user workflow if there is anything not clear the user would find along the path of using the export or if anything blows. 

@rnkazman has been waiting for this capability since 2023 (!). I think you have all the parts to do this. DV8 has built in capability to do this via code, commit and dependencies, but exporting an author graph to run the clustering designed for source code would be a first, and it would quite literally give us a new "community detection OSLOM" by using DV8 DRSpace to cluster the graphs.

I understand this is a big ask, so you can just try going as far as it doesn't take a lot of time. It may give us a good way to check for bottlenecks where things needs to be made easier (possibly a notebook on Fall building on your work).
